      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Rosai-Dorfman disease was commonly characterized as massive, painless, bilateral, symmetric cervical lymphadenopathy, with fever, leukocytosis, and elevated sedimentation rate. However, soft tissue Rosai-Dorfman disease (STRDD) is a rare benign tumor."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We hereby present 1 case of a 17-month-old girl, an isolated subcutaneous mass was detected on her right forearm, and no signs of pain, swelling, or erythema were observed at the site."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The patient underwent an excisional biopsy for the mass. Immunohistochemistry results showed that it was positive for S-100 protein and CD68, whereas negative for CD1a, which supported the diagnosis of STRDD."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The patient showed no evidence of recurrence or metastasis 2 years after the surgery.Some multifocal masses were reported to be much more prone to recurrence. Further follow-up of STRDD is necessary."
        }
      ]},
